@@ -142,6 +142,8 @@ Offline novel files are deliberately excluded, matching J2K's treatment of downl
 
 Hayai's Manga/Novels search scope remains attached to J2K's new global-search entry path, including searches restored with filters. Upstream owns saved-search storage and UI. Hayai's old source presets migrate into it only when the current source filter schema can represent their complete state; unresolved entries remain durable migration input. No second active saved-search writer or dialog remains. Both the upstream preferences and pending legacy entries use the existing app-settings backup path. Core and Hayai database schemas are unchanged.
 
+The upstream `MainActivity` changes remain identical to the new parent. No new adapter is added to `App.kt`, `MainActivity.kt`, or the image `ReaderActivity.kt`. Duplicate chapter cleanup retains J2K's download authority and bookmark protection while applying the same category exclusions as ordinary read-based deletion.
+
 The first October integration build confirmed that JitPack no longer provides J2K's pinned arkon FlexibleAdapter `c8013533` artifacts. Tsundoku `775508f3c6e8814ab38634874168948600e81574`, referencing Mihon `737fdbfbf`, switches to the canonical Maven Central artifact. Hayai follows that packaging correction for core 5.1.0 and UI 1.0.0 in `app/build.gradle.kts`, retaining the existing adapter API and ownership. Local caches are not a release dependency.
 
 The canonical adapter automatically filters updates when a query exists. J2K's details and category adapters already produce filtered datasets, including its blank library placeholder. The existing `MangaDetailsAdapter` seam and new `LibraryCategoryAdapter` seam retain the superclass UPDATE path for those prepared lists while preserving the stored query for highlighting. This also keeps same-query refreshes and existing headers and footers. Browse and other adapters retain the dependency's normal filter behavior.
